    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/disability-adviser-temporary-cover-aw50599": {
      "title": "Your cookie choices",
      "description": "Disability Adviser [Temporary Cover] | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Disability Adviser [Temporary Cover] Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Disability Adviser [Temporary Cover] Temporary cover: This post is fixed-term until 26 April 2027 or the return of the post holder, whichever is the earlier. The Accessibility & Disability Resource Centre (ADRC) is seeking to appoint a Disability Adviser to join its Disability Advisory Team. As a Disability Adviser, you will provide specialist advice, guidance, and support to prospective and current disabled students, as well as to colleagues who support disabled students across the University. You will work collaboratively with academic departments, Colleges, professional service teams, and other stakeholders to facilitate inclusive learning environments and ensure students receive appropriate support throughout their studies. Reporting to the Senior Adviser, Disability Advisory Team, you will manage a diverse caseload, develop support recommendations, and contribute to the delivery and continuous improvement of the University's disability support services. You will hold a degree (or possess equivalent relevant professional experience). A postgraduate qualification in a disability-related field and professional accreditation (such as membership of the National Association of Disability Practitioners (NADP) would be advantageous. The successful candidate will have recent experience of advising disabled students within a higher education or comparable setting, together with a thorough understanding of disability legislation, regulatory guidance, and current practice relating to disability inclusion in tertiary education. Knowledge of, and a commitment to, the principles of Universal Design for Learning (UDL) would be highly desirable. You will possess excellent written and verbal communication skills, with the ability to build effective working relationships with individuals from a wide range of backgrounds and disciplines. You will be organised, able to prioritise competing demands, and comfortable working to deadlines within a busy and dynamic student support environment. A strong commitment to equality, inclusion, and student-centred practice is essential. Benefits of working at the University of Cambridge include: Competitive rates of pay with automatic service-related pay progression and annual cost of living increases; Generous annual leave allowance; Flexible and hybrid working opportunities; Generous maternity, adoption and shared parental leave entitlement and other family friendly schemes (e.g. workplace nurseries); An auto-enrolment pension scheme, with a generous employer contribution; Travel benefits and retail discounts at over 2,000 local and national stores; Schemes to support with relocation / the provision of accommodation Further information, including guidance for disabled applicants, is available in the Further Particulars document. Additional information regarding the role can also be found within the Further Particulars. When applying, please upload your CV and cover letter which clearly sets out how you meet the criteria listed in the person specification of the Further Particulars (the knowledge, skills, and experience required for the role) along with relevant examples. Candidates will be shortlisted and invited to interview based upon these criteria. Applications are welcome from external and internal candidates, including internal candidates who would like to apply for the role on the basis of a secondment from their current role in the University. Informal enquiries are welcome and should be directed to Daniel McFarlane, Senior Disability Adviser at dm2080@cam.ac.uk The closing date for applications is: 06/09/2026 The interview date for the role is: 17/09/2026 Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. If you have any queries regarding the application process please contact SSCjobs@admin.cam.ac.uk Please quote reference AW50599 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Student Support Salary £35,608-£46,049 Reference AW50599 Category Academic-related Date published 7 August 2026 Closing date 6 September 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
