    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/natural-history-engagement-coordinator-fixed-term-pe50868": {
      "title": "Natural History Engagement Coordinator (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Natural History Engagement Coordinator (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Natural History Engagement Coordinator (Fixed Term) Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Natural History Engagement Coordinator (Fixed Term) We are looking for an enthusiastic and well-connected Natural History Engagement Coordinator to join Collections-Connections-Communities (CCC), a Strategic Research Initiative at the University of Cambridge which empowers researchers to use the University's collections to address the big questions of our time. Cambridge holds one of the world's most significant natural history collections ¿ internationally important zoological, botanical, geological and palaeontological specimens and living collections, alongside related archives, rare books and artworks. This is a fixed-term post within a new Collections and Participation team, funded for one year in the first instance, with potential extension to July 2030, working alongside a Programmes Coordinator and two Research Associates. You will coordinate a varied programme of engagement and training activities based on these collections and the research around them, broadening access for external audiences, communities and partners. You will co-design and deliver training and learning programmes, workshops, seminars and resources that build capacity, working with natural history collections, biodiversity data and living collections. You will build relationships with museums, botanic gardens, herbaria, conservation organisations, libraries and archives, coordinate networks and communities of practice connecting collection professionals, researchers, educators and policymakers, and support collaborative projects that ensure our collections-based research will impact on the challenges of biodiversity, environmental change and sustainability. We're looking for someone with experience of developing, coordinating or delivering externally focused programmes, events, workshops or training activities, and of working with natural history collections, museums, botanic gardens, herbaria or biodiversity organisations. An understanding of the role of these collections in research, conservation, education and innovation, and of public engagement and knowledge exchange in a research or cultural environment, is essential, as are excellent communication and networking skills, strong project coordination skills, and a degree or equivalent professional experience in biodiversity, natural sciences or a natural history discipline. Knowledge of other collection domains, familiarity with digitisation or biodiversity informatics, experience of designing learning programmes for a range of ages, and a postgraduate or teaching qualification would be an advantage. This is a chance to work across a collections ecosystem of exceptional depth, where the most interesting opportunities often emerge in the connections between a specimen, a living plant, an archive and a rare book. The networks you build will help maximise the value of collections that speak directly to biodiversity loss and environmental change. Occasional evening and weekend working, and occasional travel within the UK and internationally, may be required. If you'd like to connect one of the world's great natural history collections with the people and organisations who need it, we'd love to hear from you. Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available for 1 years in the first instance. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. For any questions relating to this recruitment please contact admin@botanic.cam.ac.uk The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Role Profile/Further information Key information Department/location Botanic Garden Salary £33,951-£39,906 Reference PE50868 Category Academic-related Date published 27 August 2026 Closing date 11 October 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/next-generation-fellow-fixed-term-pm50284": {
      "title": "Next Generation Fellow (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Next Generation Fellow (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Next Generation Fellow (Fixed Term) Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Next Generation Fellow (Fixed Term) The Loke Centre for Trophoblast Research (CTR) was founded in 2007 as a Centre of excellence to promote scientific study of the placenta, early development and maternal-fetal interactions during pregnancy. It brings together over 30 Principal Investigators, many of whom are recognized international experts in their fields. The Loke CTR has an opening for a Next Generation Fellow who will complement and synergise with our existing research programmes. Next Generation Fellowships are a springboard to a long and successful career in the field of placental and reproductive biology and highly flexible to suit individual needs, to enable the postholder to develop as an independent researcher. Holders are expected, and will be supported, to apply for Research Fellowship/Career Development Awards from research councils or other funders, or a faculty position during their tenure. Find out more about the fellowship on our website: https://www.trophoblast.cam.ac.uk/ngf-overview The Next Generation Fellowships are a springboard to a long and successful career in the field of placental and reproductive biology. Many of our Next Generation Fellows have used the opportunity as a stepping stone to independent group leader positions: https://www.trophoblast.cam.ac.uk/next-generation-fellow-alumni . What to expect Next Generation Fellows will be mentored to develop and establish an independent research program that will complement that of other groups within the Centre for Trophoblast Research. Fellows will be expected to contribute fully to the activities of the Loke CTR and to their host department. They will supervise MPhil/masters, summer and/or undergraduate students. They may be a co-applicant on research grant applications to recruit, train and co-supervise postdoctoral researchers, research assistants, technical staff and PhD students. The post holder will be expected to create a strong track record of publishing high impact papers and disseminate the results of their work in any ways that seem appropriate and effective (e.g. via conferences and seminars). They will be responsible for compliance with all the relevant laws (Home Office, HTA, HFEA). Candidates should hold a PhD and have a high-quality publication track record in academic journals or equivalent.  We welcome applications from across a diverse range of research relevant to our Grand Challenges https://www.trophoblast.cam.ac.uk/about-us/objectives . Contract details Appointments will be made on a fixed-term, full-time, basis for a period of 3 years (Grade 7, £37,694- £46,049 for appointments in 2027). There is a possibility of up to 1-year extension. The contract further includes research consumables/equipment of £60,000 and travel support up to £750 over the duration of the fellowship. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. How to apply To apply to this post, candidates should first identify a research Sponsor from the list of Principal Investigators https://www.trophoblast.cam.ac.uk/people/principal-investigators within the Centre for Trophoblast Research, and discuss their research plans with them. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. In addition to this, please ensure that you upload the application documentations as follows: Curriculum Vitae (CV) Up to 3 letters of reference Letter of support from your chosen Loke CTR sponsor Loke CTR Next Generation Fellowship application form Closing date: midnight on 31st January 2027. Please quote reference PM50284 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Application Form Further Particulars Key information Department/location Department of Physiology, Development and Neuroscience Salary £37,694-£46,049 Reference PM50284 Category Research Date published 15 July 2026 Closing date 31 January 2027 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
