    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/phd-studentship-in-theory-and-practice-of-high-dynamic-range-imaging-fixed-term-nr50548": {
      "title": "Your cookie choices",
      "description": "PhD Studentship in Theory and Practice of High Dynamic Range Imaging (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs PhD Studentship in Theory and Practice of High Dynamic Range Imaging (Fixed Term) Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs PhD Studentship in Theory and Practice of High Dynamic Range Imaging (Fixed Term) Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available for 3.5 years. Dive into the future of visual technology with a studentship at the forefront of modern imaging science, where high dynamic range (HDR) imaging is redefining the way smartphone cameras and displays capture the world. Despite HDR becoming the new standard, many classic image-processing algorithms and generative models remain limited to the world of standard dynamic range (SDR). This project seeks to bridge that divide, unifying methods and technologies designed for both SDR and HDR content-spanning still images and video alike. You'll explore the exciting frontiers of HDR multispectral imaging and imaging with non-standard primaries, pushing the boundaries of colour accuracy and personalising colour reproduction for all, including those with colour vision deficiencies. The work doesn't stop there: you'll also investigate advanced tone-mapping and inverse-tone-mapping techniques to translate seamlessly between HDR and SDR worlds, ensuring consistent colour and contrast perception across luminance levels. Building on cutting-edge psychophysical models, this research promises to reshape how we see, capture, and reproduce the visual world. In brief, the topics of interest include: Principled methods for adapting SDR algorithms to HDR content [1,2] Multispectral HDR imaging, with the focus on colour accuracy and colour reproduction that accounts for the differences in colour perception [3,4] Mapping between SDR and HDR that preserves colour and contrast appearance, and relies on psychophysical models of human vision [5-9] You will work with both machine learning and psychophysical models of human vision [5,9,10]. The study may also involve psychophysical experimentation to collect novel data on human colour vision and image quality [10] This project is a collaboration between Prof. Rafal Mantiuk (University of Cambridge) and Prof. Graham Finlayson (The Colour & Imaging Lab, The University of East Anglia).  Profs Mantiuk and Finlayson have a long track record of working in HDR Imaging, Displays, and Psychophysical evaluation, [1,2,3,4,5,6]. This project is funded by a donation through Apple Inc. (Cambridge). It is expected that the successful student will intern with Apple during one Summer of the PhD. The project is based at the University of Cambridge. Applicants must contact Prof. Rafal Mantiuk ( rafal.mantiuk@cl.cam.ac.uk ) in advance to assess topic and background fit. Please include a CV and a 2-paragraph research statement that shows evidence of engagement with this advert. Further information on the PhD in Computer Science programme can be found at: https://www.postgraduate.study.cam.ac.uk/courses/directory/cscspdpcs/apply All applications should be made online via the University's Applicant Portal: https://www.postgraduate.study.cam.ac.uk/courses/directory/cscspdpcs/apply . Please quote the reference NR50548 in the Research Topic so that applications can be routed directly to Prof. Mantiuk. Applications should include academic transcripts, a CV, a research proposal, and 2 references. An application is only complete when all supporting documents, including the 2 academic references, are submitted. It is your responsibility to ensure that both referees submit their references before the closing date. The research proposal should expand on at least two topics listed in the bullet points above. This studentship provides full approved tuition fees and maintenance at recommended UKRI rates.  Both home and overseas students are welcome to apply. It is expected that the selected candidate will start in January 2027. We encourage groups currently underrepresented in Engineering and Physical Science subjects. Amongst UK-domiciled students, this includes women, Black British, British Bangladeshi and British Pakistani applicants. Amongst UK-domiciled and international applicants, we also particularly welcome applications from people from low-income backgrounds, mature students, care-experienced students, and students from families where no parent or care-giver went to university. Further information can be found on our widening participation webpages https://www.postgraduate.study.cam.ac.uk/apply/before/widening-access [1] A. Y. Ke et al., 'Training Neural Networks on RAW and HDR Images for Restoration Tasks', New Trends in Image Restoration and Enhancement (CVPR workshop), 2025.\n[2] R. K. Mantiuk and M. Azimi, 'PU21: A novel perceptually uniform encoding for adapting existing quality metrics for HDR', in 2021 Picture Coding Symposium (PCS), IEEE, June 2021, pp. 1-5. doi: 10.1109/PCS50896.2021.9477471.\n[3] G. D. Finlayson and Y. Zhu, 'Designing Color Filters That Make Cameras More Colorimetric', IEEE Trans. on Image Process., vol. 30, pp. 853-867, 2021, doi: 10.1109/TIP.2020.3038523.\n[4] H. Gao, R. K. Mantiuk, and G. D. Finlayson, 'Color-Accurate Camera Capture with Multispectral Illumination and Multiple Exposures', Computer Graphics Forum, vol. 43, no. 7, p. e15252, Oct. 2024, doi: 10.1111/cgf.15252.\n[5] R. Mantiuk, S. Daly, and L. Kerofsky, 'Display adaptive tone mapping', ACM Transactions on Graphics (Proc. of SIGGRAPH), vol. 27, no. 3, p. 68, 2008, doi: 10.1145/1360612.1360667.\n[6] J. Vazquez-Corral, G.D. Finlayson and L. Herranz, \"Improving the perception of low-light enhanced images\", Optics Express, 5174-5190, 2024.\n[7] G.D. Finlayson and J. McVey, \"TM-Net: A Neural Net Architecture for Tone Mapping\", Journal of Imaging, 325, 2022. \n[8] S. Westland, G.D. Finlayson, P. Lai, Q. Pan, J. Yang and Y. Chen. \"A computational method for predicting color palette discriminability\", Color Research & Application, 2024\n[9] G. Eilertsen, J. Kronander, G. Denes, R. K. Mantiuk, and J. Unger, 'HDR image reconstruction from a single exposure using deep CNNs', ACM Transaction on Graphics, vol. 36, no. 6, p. Article 178, 2017.\n[10] Supra-threshold Contrast Perception in Augmented Reality Dongyeon Kim, Maliha Ashraf, Alexandre Chapiro, Rafal K. Mantiuk. In: Proc. of SIGGRAPH Asia 2025, 2025 Please quote reference NR50548 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Key information Department/location Department of Computer Science and Technology Salary Reference NR50548 Category Studentships Date published 28 July 2026 Closing date 4 September 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/post-doctoral-research-associate-in-experimental-quantum-optics-and-quantum-light-spectroscopy-fixed-ma50619": {
      "title": "Post-Doctoral Research Associate in Experimental Quantum Optics and Quantum-Light Spectroscopy (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Post-Doctoral Research Associate in Experimental Quantum Optics and Quantum-Light Spectroscopy (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Post-Doctoral Research Associate in Experimental Quantum Optics and Quantum-Light Spectroscopy (Fixed Term) Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Post-Doctoral Research Associate in Experimental Quantum Optics and Quantum-Light Spectroscopy (Fixed Term) Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available for 34 months in the first instance. Applications are invited for a Post-Doctoral Research Associate to join the group of Dr Raj Pandya in the Department of Chemistry at the University of Cambridge. The position is funded for 34 months through the UK-US project REACT-Q (entanglement tRansfEr And Correlations in polaritonic chemisTry with Quantum light), undertaken in close collaboration with Professor Milan Delor's group at Columbia University. The project asks whether non-classical properties of light, including photon correlations, squeezing and entanglement, can be transferred to and preserved within molecular exciton-polaritons in on-chip waveguides, and how those correlations evolve during chemically relevant processes such as singlet fission. The successful applicant will lead the Cambridge experimental quantum-optics programme. They will develop and characterise entangled-photon-pair and bright squeezed vacuum sources; perform photon-correlation measurements including g(2) and Hanbury Brown-Twiss measurements; undertake Hong-Ou-Mandel interference and joint spectral correlation/entanglement measurements; and couple non-classical light into molecularly clad photonic waveguides. They will quantify the effects of matter fraction, propagation length, loss, disorder and molecular cladding on photon statistics, spectral correlations and two-photon indistinguishability, and contribute to quantum pump-probe measurements of singlet fission. The role will involve close collaboration with Columbia University, including exchange of samples and data and research visits to Columbia, as well as preparation of publications, presentation of results, and support for the wider activities of the collaboration. Applicants must have, or be close to obtaining, a PhD in experimental quantum optics, quantum photonics, photonics, physics, physical chemistry, electrical engineering, or a closely related field. Hands-on experience in quantum optics, photonics, or a related area of optical experimentation is essential. Experience in one or more of the following would be highly desirable: Hong-Ou-Mandel interference; second-order photon-correlation [g(2)] or coincidence measurements; SPDC or other non-classical light sources; spectral-correlation or entanglement measurements; bright squeezed vacuum or other squeezed/multimode sources; single-photon detectors and timing electronics; and coupling or characterising light in integrated photonic waveguides. Candidates are not expected to have prior experience in all of these areas, and training will be provided in techniques that are new to them. Good optical alignment, data-analysis and scientific-programming skills are essential. Experience of molecular exciton-polaritons, silicon photonics, quantum-state reconstruction, or international collaborative research would be a bonus but are not required. The applicant will be expected to work independently while contributing actively to a highly interdisciplinary UK-US team spanning quantum optics, ultrafast spectroscopy, nanophotonics and chemical physics. Excellent communication, organisational and collaborative skills are required. Appointment at Research Associate level is dependent on having obtained a PhD; applicants who have submitted but not yet received their PhD may initially be appointed at Research Assistant level and promoted upon award of the PhD, in accordance with University policy. We welcome applications from individuals who wish to be considered for part-time working or other flexible working arrangements. We particularly welcome applications from women and /or candidates from a BME background for this vacancy as they are currently under-represented at this level in our department. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please ensure that you upload your Curriculum Vitae (CV), a covering letter and publications list in the upload section of the online application. If you upload any additional documents that have not been requested, we will not be able to consider these as part of your application. Please contact rp558@cam.ac.uk for informal enquiries. Please quote reference MA50619 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The Department holds an Athena SWAN silver award for women in Science, Technology, Engineering, Mathematics, and Medicine. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further information Key information Department/location Yusuf Hamied Department of Chemistry Salary £34,610-£35,608 £37,694- £46,049 Reference MA50619 Category Research Date published 4 August 2026 Closing date 8 September 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/post-doctoral-research-associate-indoor-air-quality-fixed-term-ma50851": {
      "title": "Post-Doctoral Research Associate: Indoor air quality (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Post-Doctoral Research Associate: Indoor air quality (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Post-Doctoral Research Associate: Indoor air quality (Fixed Term) Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Post-Doctoral Research Associate: Indoor air quality (Fixed Term) Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available for 1 year in the first instance. Applications are invited for a post-doctoral research associate to join the group of Professor Chiara Giorio at the Yusuf Hamied Department of Chemistry to work on indoor air quality in homes within the framework of the INQUIRE project, a EU-Horizon Europe-funded research and innovation action involving 20 institutions across Europe and Australia. INQUIRE investigates the chemical and biological determinants of indoor air quality (IAQ) in over 200 European homes with infants and young children, with the aim of protecting citizen health and informing future IAQ regulation. You will lead UCAM's activities within Task 5.2 (\"Associations between IAQ, Internal Exposure and Human Health\"), working alongside co-leads at other European institutes and other WP1¿WP4 partners generating the underlying air quality, biomonitoring, and toxicity data. Key responsibilities: Lead UCAM's contribution to Task 5.2, including modelling of respiratory deposition of indoor particulate matter and estimation of exposure to aerosol-bound chemicals, using measurement data generated in WP2 (chemical/biological profiling) and WP3 (toxicity assessment). Apply lung deposition modelling approaches (accounting for age, gender, activity level, and respiratory tract disease status) to translate ambient/indoor concentration data into internal dose estimates. Contribute to statistical analyses linking IAQ and human biomonitoring (HBM) data to self-reported health outcomes and questionnaire-derived exposure determinants (e.g. socioeconomic status, building characteristics, product use). Collaborate with WP1 (sampling/biomonitoring), WP2 (chemical/biological characterisation) and WP3 (toxicity) partners to ensure appropriate integration of exposure and effect data. Contribute findings to INQUIRE's cross-project synthesis and to the \"INQUIRE Action Plan for Promoting Healthy Homes in Europe\". Prepare data for deposition in FAIR repositories and co-author scientific publications and stakeholder-facing outputs. Contribute to INQUIRE reporting requirements. Present results at INQUIRE consortium meetings (General Assembly, WP5 workshops) and relevant international conferences. Contribute to supervision of any associated students/research assistants at UCAM working on related tasks. Essential requirements: PhD (or near completion) in environmental science, exposure science, aerosol/atmospheric science, epidemiology, biostatistics, or a closely related field. Strong quantitative/statistical skills and experience handling large, multi-source environmental and/or biomonitoring datasets. Proficiency in a relevant programming/statistical environment (e.g. R, Python, or MATLAB). Excellent written and verbal communication skills, with the ability to work effectively within a large, multidisciplinary, multinational consortium. Willingness to travel occasionally within Europe for consortium meetings. Desirable: Demonstrable experience in exposure modelling and/or air pollution health-effects research (e.g. particle deposition modelling, dosimetry, or exposure¿response analysis). Experience with human biomonitoring data or epidemiological cohort data (e.g. birth cohort studies). Familiarity with indoor air quality sensor data and/or suspect/non-target chemical screening datasets. Experience with FAIR data principles and open-science data management. Prior experience within EU Horizon/H2020-funded consortium projects. Anticipated Start Date: as soon as possible after recruitment process is completed (e.g., September/October 2026) We welcome applications from individuals who wish to be considered for part-time working or other flexible working arrangements. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please ensure that you upload your Curriculum Vitae (CV), a covering letter and publications list in the upload section of the online application. If you upload any additional documents that have not been requested, we will not be able to consider these as part of your application. For queries relating to your application or the application process, please contact Prof Chiara Giorio via email on cg525@cam.ac.uk . Please quote reference MA50851 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The Department holds an Athena SWAN silver award for women in Science, Technology, Engineering, Mathematics, and Medicine. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further information Key information Department/location Yusuf Hamied Department of Chemistry Salary £34,610  £35,608 £37,694  £46,049 Reference MA50851 Category Research Date published 25 August 2026 Closing date 15 September 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/postdoctoral-research-associate-evaluating-food-marketing-restrictions-fixed-term-sj50788": {
      "title": "Postdoctoral Research Associate (evaluating food marketing restrictions) [Fixed Term]",
      "description": "Postdoctoral Research Associate (evaluating food marketing restrictions) [Fixed Term] | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Postdoctoral Research Associate (evaluating food marketing restrictions) [Fixed Term] Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Postdoctoral Research Associate (evaluating food marketing restrictions) [Fixed Term] Postdoctoral Research Associate evaluating food marketing restrictions, Population Health Interventions Group, Institute of Metabolic Science (IMS) Epidemiology IMS Epidemiology is seeking to appoint a Postdoctoral Research Associate with skills and experience in quantitative methods for evaluation of natural experiments to contribute to a multi-component evaluation of new restrictions on marketing of less healthy foods. New restrictions on advertising of less healthy food and drinks online and on TV before 9pm were implemented in the UK in January 2026. Restrictions on price-based promotions of less healthy products in England were implemented in October 2025. Based in the Population Health Interventions Group, the primary role of this post is to lead quantitative analyses exploring the impact of the new restrictions on: 1. household grocery purchasing, using data we will purchase from Worldpanel by Numerator; and 2. nutritional content of foods served by chain restaurants, using data from our MenuTracker database. We anticipate that both pieces of work will use of interrupted time series analysis methods. Funded by the National Institute of Health Research (NIHR), we are conducting a multi-component evaluation of the restrictions. Across the whole evaluation, we will study impacts on marketing, availability and purchasing of less healthy products; model impacts on health and wider society; explore awareness and acceptability; and interrogate policy processes. The work is led from IMS Epidemiology and involves colleagues at the Universities of Oxford, Stirling, Exeter, Waterloo and the London School of Hygiene and Tropical Medicine. You will be educated to PhD level in a relevant subject or due to complete within 3 months of applying. You will have proven skills and experience in quantitative methods for evaluation of natural experiments, such as interrupted time series or difference-in-difference analysis. A complete list of essential and desirable criteria for this post is provided in the further particulars. Your application is unlikely to be competitive if it does not provide clear and concise evidence that you meet all of the essential criteria. Alongside a competitive salary, University staff are eligible for a comprehensive package of benefits and services including discounts on shopping, private health care, financial services and public transport. We also offer defined benefits pension schemes and tax-efficient bicycle, car lease and charity-giving schemes. This is a fixed-term position for 21 months. The role can be performed as a hybrid position, recognising that some tasks may only be performed on University premises. Please contact Professor Jean Adams, jma79@medschl.cam.ac.uk for informal enquiries. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Applicants must have (due to complete within 3 months of applying) a PhD. Appointment at Research Associate level is dependent on having a PhD. Those who have submitted but not yet received their PhD will initially be appointed as a Research Assistant (Grade 5) moving to Research Associate (Grade 7) upon confirmation of your PhD award. Please ensure that you upload a covering letter and CV in the Upload section of the online application. The covering letter should outline how you match the criteria for the post and why you are applying for this role. If you upload any additional documents which have not been requested, we will not be able to consider these as part of your application. Please include details of your referees, including email address and phone number, one of which must be your most recent line manager. Closing Date: 14th September 2026 Interview Date: 29th or 30th September 2026 Please quote reference SJ50788 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Clinical Biochemistry (Institute of Metabolic Science Epidemiology) Salary £37,694-£46,049 Reference SJ50788 Category Research Date published 19 August 2026 Closing date 14 September 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
