    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/research-assistantresearch-associate-fixed-term-pm50828": {
      "title": "Research Assistant/Research Associate (fixed term)",
      "description": "Research Assistant/Research Associate (fixed term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Research Assistant/Research Associate (fixed term) Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Research Assistant/Research Associate (fixed term) Applications are invited for a postdoctoral research associate in the lab of Dr Jasper Poort, based at the Department of Physiology, Development and Neuroscience at the University of Cambridge. The successful candidate will work on a research project that will investigate the neural mechanisms of visual decision-making, learning, and attention. The research combines electrophysiology, and optogenetic manipulation of different cell types and neural projections in visual and decision-making brain areas to understand how mice learn to become experts in different visually-guided decision-making tasks and flexibly switch attention between tasks. The successful applicant will join a supportive and multi-disciplinary research environment including experimental facilities and expertise in the Poort lab ( https://www.pdn.cam.ac.uk/svl/ ) and collaborators. Applicants should have completed (or are about to submit) a PhD (research associate) or (under)graduate degree (research assistant) in neuroscience, biology, or other relevant disciplines. We are looking for someone with previous experience in chronic extracellular electrophysiology (e.g. tetrodes or multichannel probes), behaviour in mice and advanced data analysis skills (e.g. Matlab or Python). Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available for 2 years in the first instance. Once an offer of employment has been accepted, the successful candidate will be required to undergo a basic disclosure (criminal records check) check and a security check. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. To apply for this post, please submit a CV, cover letter, and a 1 page research proposal on 'circuit mechanisms of visually-guided decision-making'. We also need the contact details of two people who can provide a professional reference. The closing date for applications is 8th September 2026. Informal enquiries about the position can be made to Dr Jasper Poort ( jp816@cam.ac.uk ). Please quote reference PM50828 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Department of Physiology, Development and Neuroscience Salary £33,002 - £35,608 or £37,694- £46,049 Reference PM50828 Category Research Date published 25 August 2026 Closing date 8 September 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/research-assistantresearch-associate-ai-tutors-for-stem-education-fixed-term-nr50921": {
      "title": "Research Assistant/Research Associate",
      "description": "Research Assistant/Research Associate - AI Tutors for STEM Education (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Research Assistant/Research Associate - AI Tutors for STEM Education (Fixed Term) Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Research Assistant/Research Associate - AI Tutors for STEM Education (Fixed Term) Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available for 2 years. We are seeking to appoint a full-time Post-Doctoral Research Associate (PDRA) to lead the design, evaluation and deployment of an AI-powered tutor within the Isaac Science and Ada Computer Science online learning platforms.\nThis is an exciting position within a well-established and successful online STEM education project and a chance to make an immediate real-world impact with your work in this fast-developing and exciting new area in the education sector. Isaac Science, developed by the University of Cambridge, and Ada Computer Science, developed in collaboration with the Raspberry Pi Foundation, use modern web technologies and evidence-based educational approaches to improve STEM education. The platform provides online learning resources to improve physics, mathematics, chemistry, biology and computer science teaching to students aged 11-18 and is used extensively by schools across the UK and internationally. The platform currently supports hundreds of thousands of students and teachers and receives millions of question attempts each month. The successful candidate will lead a major new initiative to develop a Socratic AI tutor capable of providing high quality, pedagogically informed support to learners as they work through questions on the platform. The AI tutor will be designed to guide student thinking through hints, prompts and explanations while deliberately avoiding the disclosure of complete answers. The objective is to enhance learning, develop student understanding and strengthen problem solving approaches. The work will include a review of existing AI tutoring and AI-assisted learning systems, including commercial products and research prototypes. The successful candidate will work alongside our dedicated software engineering team to design and implement a prototype AI tutor as well as collaborate with our content and education teams where they will undertake trials with partner schools, evaluate learning outcomes and user experiences, and contribute to the deployment of successful approaches at scale across Isaac Science and Ada Computer Science. Research Environment The PDRA will be based within the Department of Computer Science and Technology at the University of Cambridge, a world-leading centre for research and innovation in computer science, education technology and computing education. The role will involve close collaboration with: The Isaac Science and Ada Computer Science software engineering team The Isaac Science content and education team Colleagues at the Raspberry Pi Foundation developing content for Ada Computer Science Subject matter experts and content developers across STEM disciplines Researchers within the Raspberry Pi Computing Education Research Centre Academic researchers in AI, learning sciences and STEM education Teachers and schools participating in platform evaluation and trials Members of the wider UK and international community exploring the responsible use of AI in schools The successful candidate will have a unique opportunity to influence the future of AI-enabled learning while working within an established and highly successful educational project in a world-class computer science department. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Key Responsibilities Conduct research into AI tutoring systems, generative AI, educational AI and related literature. Evaluate existing commercial and research AI tutoring products and identify best practice. Design frameworks for pedagogically effective AI-generated feedback and hints. Lead the design of an AI tutor for Isaac Science and Ada Computer Science. Work closely with software engineers to implement and test prototype systems. Develop approaches to ensure AI-generated responses support learning without directly providing answers. Design and conduct evaluations, pilot studies and school-based trials. Analyse quantitative and qualitative evidence from platform usage and educational studies. Disseminate findings through academic publications, conferences, reports and stakeholder engagement. Contribute to grant proposals and future research initiatives where appropriate. Collaborate effectively with academic, technical and educational partners. Contribute to the wider research activities of the Department. A complete list of requirements and desirable criteria for the role may be found in the further particulars document.  Examples of the essential criteria for this role include: A PhD (or be close to completion) in computer science, artificial intelligence, machine learning, human-computer interaction, education, learning sciences, educational technology, computing education or another directly relevant discipline. Experience of educational technology research or development. Experience designing or evaluating digital learning interventions What we offer Conduct impactful research in one of the most significant emerging areas of educational technology. Shape how AI is used to support learning in schools. Work with a highly experienced team of researchers, software engineers and subject experts. Collaborate with leading organisations, including the Raspberry Pi Foundation. Access established networks of partner schools ready to participate in pilot studies and evaluations. Publish impactful research while contributing to a widely used educational platform. Be part of a successful, long-running University of Cambridge project with a strong record of innovation and impact. Contribute to teaching and mentoring activities within the Department where appropriate. In addition, the University offers an excellent working environment, generous annual leave entitlement, membership of the USS pension scheme, extensive professional development opportunities and flexible working arrangements. Appointment at the Research Associate level is dependent on the award of a PhD. This is a postdoctoral-level appointment, those who have submitted but not yet received their PhD will be appointed at the Research Assistant -Grade 5, which will be amended to Research Associate-Grade 7 once the award of the PhD has been conferred. Further information For more information about the platforms, please visit: https://isaacscience.org https://adacomputerscience.org Informal enquiries are welcome and may be directed to: Professor Alastair Beresford arb33@cam.ac.uk Applications should include a curriculum vitae, a covering letter describing relevant experience and interest in the role and contact details for two academic referees. Please quote reference NR50921 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. Apply online Download further information Further information Key information Department/location Department of Computer Science and Technology Salary £34,610  £35,608 £37,694  £46,049 Reference NR50921 Category Research Date published 2 September 2026 Closing date 16 September 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/research-assistantresearch-associate-applied-zero-knowledge-proof-systems-nr50856": {
      "title": "Research Assistant/Research Associate",
      "description": "Research Assistant/Research Associate - Applied Zero-Knowledge Proof Systems | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Research Assistant/Research Associate - Applied Zero-Knowledge Proof Systems Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Research Assistant/Research Associate - Applied Zero-Knowledge Proof Systems Fixed term: The post is available immediately, and funds for this post are available until 31 December 2027 (an extension may be possible). How can mutually untrusting AI agents, perhaps representing different companies, convince each other that they are trustworthy? How can one convince the other that it is not a fraudster, that it is telling the truth, that it is capable of delivering what it promises, and that it will abide by any contract it enters into? The thesis of the Centre for Cryptographic Trust Infrastructure (CCTI), recently funded by the Advanced Research + Invention Agency (ARIA), is that cryptographic protocols (zero-knowledge proofs, secure multiparty computation) and trusted hardware (TEEs, tamper-resistant sensors) will enable initially untrusting agents to establish trust by proving facts about themselves to each other, without revealing sensitive information. Our approach combines zero-knowledge proofs with cryptographic attestations about the physical world, and with game-theoretic/economic models of agents' behaviour. We are looking for an experienced researcher in the area of cryptographic arguments of knowledge and their applications. The post is based at the University of Cambridge Department of Computer Science and Technology. It is part of CCTI, an international, interdisciplinary collaboration between academic, industry, and nonprofit partners that connects deep technical expertise with exciting real-world use cases. Potential projects include (but are not limited to): Designing, implementing, and verifying the cryptographic protocols for a database that can prove in zero knowledge that its responses to queries are correct (using authenticated data structures and verifiable query processing)\n-Working with game theory researchers on cryptographic commitments of agent reasoning, such as proving in zero knowledge that an action was rational under a privately held belief, and that the belief evolved according to a committed update rule, Improving mechanisms for establishing the authenticity of data, for example by proving that it was obtained from a server with a particular TLS certificate, Adversarial analysis of agent-to-agent protocols: what a sequence of proofs, queries, and refusals reveals about the parties, even when every individual message is zero-knowledge Role requirements: A PhD in Computer Science or a closely related discipline, or an equivalent level of research experience. Research experience in designing, analysing, implementing cryptographic protocols, Deep familiarity with modern zero-knowledge proof systems and their composition, for example zk-SNARKs, commit-and-prove constructions, proof-carrying data, proof recursion and/or folding schemes If you have any questions regarding the role or the application process, please contact Martin Kleppmann ( martin.kleppmann@cst.cam.ac.uk ). Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Questions about the post and the recruitment process may be addressed to the HR Team at hr-admin@cst.cam.ac.uk Please quote reference NR50856 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Department of Computer Science and Technology Salary £34,610  £35,608 £37,694  £46,049 Reference NR50856 Category Research Date published 25 August 2026 Closing date 3 September 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/research-associate-fixed-term-pm50266": {
      "title": "Research Associate (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Research Associate (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Research Associate (Fixed Term) Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Research Associate (Fixed Term) We seek a highly motivated Postdoctoral Research Associate to join the laboratory of Professor Kathy Niakan. We are based in the Loke Centre for Trophoblast Research (LCTR), in the Department of Physiology, Development and Neuroscience. A successful candidate will help to develop pioneering research projects to provide fundamental insights into human biology and inform novel strategies for stem cell biology and post-implantation development of human embryos. The aims of research projects in the lab are to characterise early lineage specification in human pre- and early post-implantation embryos. We seek to explore a range of methods to establish robust and reproducible cutting-edge single cell molecular analysis, imaging, functional genomics techniques and stem cell models to understand early lineage specification mechanisms in human embryos. Our laboratory aims to utilise the knowledge gained from these studies to provide fundamental insights into human biology. We also aim to use this knowledge to facilitate the development of robust and reproducible implantation models, novel human stem cells and stem cell-based models of human development. We perform comparative analysis of human embryogenesis to several other mammalian species, pluripotent stem cells and integrated stem cell models of human embryos. The successful candidate will be energetic, focused, and productive with a desire to work in a congenial, dynamic, and collaborative research environment. Excellent organizational, analytical, and communication skills are essential. Candidates should hold a PhD in a relevant subject. Please refer to the person specification for a full list of essential skills and qualifications. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The Department of Physiology, Development & Neuroscience particularly encourages women and candidates from a Black, Asian or Minority Ethnic background to apply for this vacancy. We will support visa application if assistance is needed. We have a legal responsibility to ensure that you have the right to work in the UK before you can start working for us. Any job application you submit to us will be assessed using criteria based on the knowledge, skills and experience required for the relevant post. You will not be treated less favourably than another applicant on the grounds of national origin. However, any offer of employment we make to you will be conditional upon you gaining permission to work in the UK. For further information: http://www.jobs.cam.ac.uk/right/have/ To find out more about the lab visit our website at: https://niakanlab.com/ To find out more about the LCTR please visit our website at: https://www.trophoblast.cam.ac.uk/ Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available for 3 years in the first instance. Once an offer of employment has been accepted, the successful candidate will be required to undergo a basic disclosure (criminal records check) check and a security check. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. To apply for this post, please submit a CV and a motivation letter detailing why you want to be part of this project and how this post will be a good stepping-stone for your career. We also need the contact details for two people who can provide a professional reference. Closing date: midnight on 13th September 2026. Please quote reference PM50266 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Department of Physiology, Development and Neuroscience Salary £33,002-£46,049 Reference PM50266 Category Research Date published 3 August 2026 Closing date 13 September 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/research-associate-fixed-term-pf50611": {
      "title": "Research Associate (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Research Associate (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Research Associate (Fixed Term) Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Research Associate (Fixed Term) A Research Associate position is available in the Social Fluids Lab, led by Dr. Adria LeBoeuf, in the Department of Zoology. The post is funded by a new 3-year international Human Frontier Science Program award investigating Socially Transferred Materials: the milks, seminal fluids, eggs, regurgitates, mucus and secretions that animals have repeatedly and convergently evolved to transfer between bodies to manipulate or support a recipient's physiology, fertility or behaviour. Despite their ecological importance and applied potential, the molecular mechanisms underlying STM function across evolutionarily distant lineages remain poorly understood. The project, joint between University of Cambridge, Vrije Universiteit Amsterdam and University of Costa Rica, combines phyloproteomics, mass-spectrometry imaging and functional bioassays across arthropods, molluscs and chordates to uncover both universal design principles of STMs and lineage-specific variants. The principal aim of this post is to lead the comparative and evolutionary bioinformatic work at the heart of the project. The approach builds on the LeBoeuf group's set-based phyloproteomic pipeline (Matte et al. bioRxiv 2025), which merges deep orthology inference with proteomic data to track molecular evolutionary shifts (convergent co-option, positive selection, duplication, loss and abundance change) within a physiological compartment. The successful candidate will use diverse proteomic datasets of STMs to identify STM proteins repeatedly co-opted across distant metazoan lineages, and generate the candidates that the wider team will test experimentally. There will also be opportunities to contribute to field sampling and to targeted wet-lab work, supported by the group's research technician. The project will involve a lot of international collaboration and travel to acquire samples from diverse organisms. A strong quantitative and computational background is essential: extensive experience in bioinformatics and scientific programming (R and/or Python), together with a solid experience in evolutionary biology and comparative phylogenetic methods. Experience working with proteomic and/or genomic datasets is essential, as is a willingness to take part in occasional field sampling and supported laboratory work. Prior hands-on experience of fieldwork, wet-lab or the generation of proteomic/genomic data, and any experience working with arthropods, would be advantageous. The successful candidate will join the group in Cambridge and become part of a new, multidisciplinary international team alongside two further postdoctoral researchers who will meet regularly online and in person, one hosted in Prof. Joris Koene's group at Vrije Universiteit Amsterdam and one in Dr. Jenny Stynoski's group at the University of Costa Rica. Close teamwork and a collaborative spirit are essential, and candidates will also be expected to demonstrate scientific independence matching their technical expertise. The role carries responsibility for compliance and permits associated with international sampling, and offers opportunities to help supervise students, contribute to project management, and publish both in large multi-author cross-system analyses and in smaller, focused papers. The nature of this role requires the successful candidate to work on site for their day-to-day duties. The primary exceptions are travel associated with fieldwork, research collaborations, conferences, and other approved activities related to the role. For more information, please refer to the Further Particulars document. Deadline for applications is September 7th. Online interviews are expected to be held in early October (subject to change). Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available for up to 12 months. Applications are welcome from internal candidates who would like to apply for the role on the basis of a secondment from their current role in the University. Part-time working or other flexible working arrangements will be considered. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Informal enquiries are welcomed and should be directed to: Dr Adria LeBoeuf [email: acl79@cam.ac.uk ] If you have any queries regarding the application process, please contact Zoology HR Office [email: hr@zoo.cam.ac.uk ] Please quote reference PF50611 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Research Associate - Further Particulars - PF50611 Key information Department/location Department of Zoology Salary £37,694-£46,049 Reference PF50611 Category Research Date published 4 August 2026 Closing date 7 September 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/research-associate-fixed-term-pf50636": {
      "title": "Research Associate (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Research Associate (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Research Associate (Fixed Term) Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Research Associate (Fixed Term) Applications are invited for a Postdoctoral Research Associate to join an exciting collaborative project, funded by the BBSRC, led by Matthias Landgraf in the Department of Zoology, University of Cambridge, ( https://www.zoo.cam.ac.uk/people/matthias-landgraf ) together with Marco Sciacovelli at the University of Liverpool ( https://www.liverpool.ac.uk/people/marco-sciacovelli ). The focus of the project are critical periods of nervous system development. These are windows of increased plasticity that enable neuronal networks to tune appropriately, based on sensory and environmental stimuli. However, when this process is perturbed, it leads to permanently maladjusted networks that affect behaviour and stability. In humans, sub-optimal critical period experiences are thought responsible for several neurodevelopmental conditions, including Schizophrenia, and forms of epilepsy. This project challenges our current understanding of the mechanistic underpinnings of critical period plasticity by proposing metabolic signals and metabolic reprogramming as central to this developmental process. The successful candidate will work with a powerful insect experimental model that we have developed: the locomotor network of the Drosophila embryo and larva. They will combine genetic and pharmacological approaches with imaging (of genetically encoded, fluorescent metabolite sensors), and integrate transcriptomics and metabolomics (targeted LC-MS and isotope tracing) data to analyse metabolic changes. Behavioural (seizure susceptibility and larval crawling) and anatomical observations (synaptic growth) will inform how metabolic reprogramming during the critical period leads to lasting phenotypic changes. We seek a new team member, based in the Department of Zoology in Cambridge, who is excited by neuroscience and how metabolism intersects with development. While primarily a \"wet-lab\" position, you should be comfortable with some bioinformatics analyses and data science of omics data. We value creativity, interdisciplinarity, curiosity, collaboration and inclusiveness. We offer a supportive, team-oriented research environment with opportunities for cutting-edge techniques and for addressing fundamental questions about how appropriate function emerges during late stages of nervous system development. For more information, please refer to the Further Particulars document Deadline for applications is midnight on 14 September 2026. Interviews are planned for the week commencing 5 October 2026 (subject to change). Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available for up to 36 months (3 years). Preferred start date between November 2026 and January 2027. Applications are welcome from internal candidates who would like to apply for the role on the basis of a secondment from their current role in the University. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Informal enquiries are welcomed and should be directed to: Professor Matthias Landgraf [email: ml10006@cam.ac.uk ] If you have any queries regarding the application process, please contact Zoology HR Office [email: hr@zoo.cam.ac.uk ] Please quote reference PF50636 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars - Research Assistant - PF50636 Key information Department/location Department of Zoology Salary £37,694-£46,049 Reference PF50636 Category Research Date published 5 August 2026 Closing date 14 September 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
