      "title": "Senior Scientific Associate (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Senior Scientific Associate (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Senior Scientific Associate (Fixed Term) Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Senior Scientific Associate (Fixed Term) Join us at the world-leading CRUK Cambridge Institute. We're a unique department of the University of Cambridge, core funded by Cancer Research UK's charitable activities, and we're eagerly searching for an experienced Senior Scientific Associate (Laboratory Manager) to join our Wang Group. The Wang Group develops and applies cutting-edge imaging and sequencing methods to bridge the gap between high resolution microscopy and genome-wide discovery. Our research program is focused on creating a quantitative, multi-scale understanding of the cell to reveal fundamental principles linking molecular-level organisation to cellular functions and pathological states. We seek an experienced, organised and collaborative individual for this role, which combines laboratory management and scientific support with hands-on experimental research. You will have the unique opportunity to take on a leadership role in a new lab, assisting Dr. Wang in managing projects in the lab, developing new scientific directions, and providing technical assistance and guidance to PhD students, postdocs and research assistants. We are interested in individuals who are flexible in leading independent projects, as well as working collaboratively within and outside of the group, and helping others when needed. You should be enthusiastic about setting up a positive lab culture, including effective teamwork, communication and organised systems (for data/code/reagents/record keeping). You should be detail-oriented and have a positive, can-do attitude to problem-solving. What you will do in this post Laboratory set-up: Plan lab layout with the Institute's estates and facilities teams; coordinate delivery, installation and commissioning of equipment; and bring the space to a state where research can begin safely and efficiently. Equipment and procurement: Specify and cost major and minor equipment, obtain quotations, place orders and negotiate with vendors; arrange service contracts, calibration and preventative maintenance; and manage faults, warranties and repairs. Safety and compliance: Own the group's health-and-safety framework, including COSHH and genetic-modification risk assessments, local rules, laser safety where applicable, waste streams and training records; act as the group's point of contact for the Institute's safety and compliance teams. Systems, data and records: Establish and maintain the group's electronic laboratory notebook, data storage and backup, code repositories, standard operating procedures, and inventories for reagents, antibodies, cell lines, consumables and samples. Budget and stock: Monitor consumable expenditure against the group's budget, place and reconcile orders, manage stock levels and minimise waste, and help the Group Leader forecast equipment and consumable spend. Induction, training and supervision: Induct every new member of the group, train them in the group's methods and standards, maintain training and competence records, and provide day-to-day supervision and technical guidance to research assistants, students and postdoctoral researchers. Core experimental platforms: Establish, validate, benchmark and document the group's core platforms so that others can run them reliably; troubleshoot failures and distinguish instrument artefact from biological signal. Your own research: Lead or co-lead experimental and method-development projects through to publication, and contribute to the group's scientific direction. Supporting the Group Leader: Contribute to project planning, prioritisation and allocation of shared resources; assist with recruitment, grant applications and funder reports; and deputise on operational matters as required. Group culture: Organise group meetings, seminars and retreats, and help build a safe, inclusive and enjoyable working environment. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available for 1 years in the first instance. Once an offer of employment has been accepted, the successful candidate will be required to undergo a basic disclosure (criminal records check) check and a security check. Please ensure that you upload a covering letter and CV in the Upload section of the online application. The covering letter should outline how you match the criteria for the post and why you are applying for this role. If you upload any additional documents which have not been requested, we will not be able to consider these as part of your application.\nPlease include details of your referees, including email address and phone number, one of which must be your most recent line manager. Closing date: 7th September 2026 Interview Date: To be Confirmed Please quote reference SW50802 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Cancer Research UK Cambridge Institute Salary £35,608-£46,049 Reference SW50802 Category Academic-related Date published 20 August 2026 Closing date 7 September 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
    "https://www.cam.ac.uk/jobs/senior-scientific-associate-fixed-term-sw50858": {
      "title": "Senior Scientific Associate (Fixed Term)",
      "description": "Senior Scientific Associate (Fixed Term) | University of Cambridge Sorry, you need to enable JavaScript to visit this website. Your cookie choices We use necessary cookies to make our site work. If you choose to accept optional cookies, it will help us understand your needs better and personalise what you see. Accept all cookies Decline optional cookies Choose your cookie mix Skip to main content View menu Study at Cambridge About the University Research at Cambridge Quick links For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our\n              researchers Business and\n              enterprise Colleges and\n              Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Search Search site Home Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Undergraduate courses Applying Events and open days Fees and finance Postgraduate Postgraduate courses How to apply Postgraduate events Fees and funding International\n                students Continuing education Lifelong learning Courses in education Close About the University How the\n                University and Colleges work Term dates and calendars History Map Visiting the University News Annual reports A global university Equality and diversity Events Public engagement Jobs Give to Cambridge Close Research at Cambridge Close For Cambridge students For staff For alumni For our researchers Business and enterprise Colleges and Departments Give to Cambridge Libraries Museums and collections Breadcrumb Home Jobs Senior Scientific Associate (Fixed Term) Jobs Menu Section - Jobs Jobs home Why Cambridge? Back to section home Why Cambridge? Why Cambridge? overview Pay and reward and employee benefits How to apply Back to section home How to apply How to apply overview Finding a role and applying online Inclusive recruitment Application tips Manage my applications Contact us Browse our vacancies Back to section home Browse our vacancies Browse our vacancies overview Academic vacancies Back to section home Academic vacancies Working as an academic at Cambridge Professional services vacancies Back to section home Professional services vacancies Working in professional services roles in Cambridge Professorships and Directorships vacancies Research vacancies Back to section home Research vacancies Working as a researcher at Cambridge Studentships Temporary employment service Search all University jobs College and affiliated institution jobs Life in Cambridge Back to section home Life in Cambridge Life in Cambridge overview Community and surroundings Getting around Relocating to Cambridge Back to section home Relocating to Cambridge Relocating to Cambridge overview Visas and immigration Right to work in the UK Accommodation and relocation support Search all University jobs Senior Scientific Associate (Fixed Term) Join the Crossley Research Group at the Cancer Research UK Cambridge Institute, where we investigate RNA-DNA hybrid molecules known as R-loops and their role in human disease. Our research focuses on understanding how R-loops contribute to genome instability and inflammation, and how these molecules and the cellular responses they trigger can be harnessed for cancer detection and treatment. To learn more about the group, visit: https://www.cruk.cam.ac.uk/research-groups/crossley-group/ We are looking for an experienced, scientifically driven and collaborative researcher to join our growing team. This role combines hands-on experimental research with opportunities to contribute to scientific support activities and laboratory operations. You will work closely with colleagues to advance projects through to publication, help establish new research directions and methodologies, and, if desired, take the lead on your own projects. You will also partner with our Senior Scientific Associate, Dr Mawer, to support the day-to-day management and smooth running of the laboratory. What You'll Be Doing Working across a range of cutting-edge research projects, you will use techniques including: Molecular biology, such as cloning, western blotting, human cell culture, CRISPR/Cas gene editing, lentiviral infection and qPCR. RNA and nucleic acid biochemistry, including RNA/DNA and RNA-DNA hybrid isolation, immunoprecipitations and pull-down assays. Immunofluorescence microscopy and flow cytometry. Genomics approaches, including next-generation sequencing (NGS) library preparation and bioinformatics analysis. About You We are particularly interested in candidates with strong molecular biology and biochemistry expertise, alongside experience in mammalian cell and/or tissue culture. You should have in-depth knowledge in at least one of the following areas: Genome stability RNA biology Chromatin biology Innate immunity Cancer biology Experience with next-generation sequencing and/or bioinformatics would be advantageous but is not essential. Beyond technical expertise, we are seeking someone who enjoys working collaboratively, both within the group and with external partners. You should be organised, detail-oriented and committed to maintaining high standards of data management, record keeping and laboratory practice. A proactive, positive approach to problem-solving and a willingness to support colleagues are highly valued. With significant long-term funding recently secured, this is an exciting opportunity to join and help shape a growing research group at an important stage of its development. Fixed-term: The funds for this post are available for 18 months in the first instance. Once an offer of employment has been accepted, the successful candidate will be required to undergo a basic disclosure (criminal records check) check, a health assessment and a security check. Click the 'Apply' button below to register an account with our recruitment system (if you have not already) and apply online. Please ensure that you upload a covering letter and a CV in the Upload section of the online application. The covering letter should outline how you match the criteria for the post and why you are applying for this role. If you upload any additional documents which have not been requested, we will not be able to consider these as part of your application. Please include details of your referees, including email address and phone number, one of which must be your most recent line manager. The closing date for applications is: 15th September 2026 The interview date for the role is: To be Confirmed Please quote reference SW50858 on your application and in any correspondence about this vacancy. The University actively supports equality, diversity and inclusion and encourages applications from all sections of society. The University has a responsibility to ensure that all employees are eligible to live and work in the UK. Apply online Download further information Further Particulars Key information Department/location Cancer Research UK Cambridge Institute Salary £37,694- £46,049 Reference SW50858 Category Academic-related Date published 1 September 2026 Closing date 15 September 2026 Connect with us Facebook Instagram LinkedIn X YouTube © 2026 University of Cambridge Contact the University Accessibility Freedom of information Privacy policy and cookies Statement on Modern Slavery Terms and conditions University A-Z Study at Cambridge Undergraduate Postgraduate Lifelong learning Courses in education About the University How the University\n              and Colleges work Give to Cambridge Jobs Map Visiting the University Cambridge University Press & Assessment Research at Cambridge Research news About research at Cambridge Public engagement"
    },
